{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3534","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3534","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3534\",\"parent_revision\":\"02f7f975a713fc2e2dad71e1d8b5749fd1f2bc7a\",\"root_revision\":\"415616ba1bb350bd7ca4024cf240d6e65a0d2e22f52420f39c3b1eb1a34026e9\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-09-13\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`.claude/skills/research-curator/references/entry-review-rubric.md` Gate 4 step 1 has exactly one outcome for a repository path that does not open: a defect recorded against the entry or proposal that named it. The rubric gives the reviewer no way to record that the path was correct when written and was broken later by a repository change.\\n\\nA cited path fails to open for two different reasons, and Gate 4 can express only one of them. A path may never have existed \u2014 the corpus holds one such case, `swarm-orchestrating`, a name inversion of the real `orchestrating-swarms`. A path may also have been correct when written and then moved or removed by a later repository change:\\n\\n- `.claude/rules/` existed until PR #3391 (`bf4dcd876`, 2026-09-04) renamed it to root-level `rules/` \u2014 59 citations.\\n- The swarm skills existed under `.claude/skills/` until PR #3422 (`4e1e73bd6`) removed them.\\n- `backlog-tools-administrator` existed until PR #3330 (`20c84f3b5`) removed it.\\n\\nConsequence: Gate 4 systematically misattributes ordinary repository refactors to the research agents that wrote the citations, and records a defect against work that was accurate at the time. Because a Gate 4 defect drives `REQUEST CHANGES`, which withholds the entry's README row (SKILL.md Post-Actions step 1), a rename elsewhere in the repo can keep correct entries out of the index. The defect class recurs on every rename or removal \u2014 it is not a closed population that goes empty after the current repair.\\n\\nWhat the gate must be able to distinguish, and does not:\\n\\n1. A path that never existed \u2014 a defect against the writer.\\n2. A path that existed when written and was moved or removed since \u2014 repository drift, a repair to schedule against the citing file, not a finding against the agent that wrote it. Recording it should name the change that moved it where that is determinable.\\n3. A path named as a place to create something \u2014 not a defect for being absent at all. (Addressed separately in PR #3532's second commit; listed here because all three outcomes live in the same step.)\\n\\nInteracts with the filing-gate consequence item: both concern when a claim is checked relative to when it is acted on. That item checks the live tree at write time, which is the only point at which drift has not happened yet; this item concerns the audit that runs afterwards, by which time it has.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: a detector shaped for recurring drift is a separate open decision, deliberately not taken inside the salvage PR. If one is designed, note that the marker gating in closed PR #3527 (`_EXISTING_STATE_MARKER` / `_NEGATION_OR_PROPOSAL_MARKER`) is what produced its 40% and 46% recall against the two known clusters \u2014 that gating is what such a checker would need to remove rather than tune, along with its scope restriction to `research/insights/` and `research/utilization/` when the affected set spans 83 files. Measurement recorded on #3527's branch and worth re-reading before designing a replacement: an ungated path scan over an entry's \\\"Relevance to Claude Code Development\\\" section measured a ~86% false-positive rate, because entries describe the researched tool's own paths in the same paragraphs as this repo's.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 salvage review of closing PRs #3527 and #3528 (PR #3532)\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-13\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"96fb62b85632e17beb27108741d2fd7a31d988260577af91afabb238efc43ad5\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVB2i1w\"}"}